is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router4
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: the inter-router uplink from Location C’s gateway (router4) to the core (RIP domain) is down. Specifically, router4’s eth5 interface toward router3 on 172.25.1.0/24 is down, so router4 is isolated.

Mechanism: PCs in 192.168.200.0/24 send traffic to Location A via their default gateway 192.168.200.1 (router4). Because router4 has no working path to the core, it immediately returns ICMP “Destination Net Unreachable,” which users observe as total loss to 172.16.10.x/20.x. Other zones still reach each other because the core (including router3) remains healthy.

Localization: 
- From pc11→pc4 (172.16.10.4): “From 192.168.200.1 Destination Net Unreachable.” 192.168.200.1 is router4.
- router4: ip link shows eth5 DOWN (172.25.1.1/24) and FRR shows only the directly connected 192.168.200.0/24 (no RIP-learned routes).
- router3: eth5 is UP (172.25.1.2/24); ping to 172.25.1.1 fails and ARP is INCOMPLETE, proving the far end (router4) isn’t responding.

Root cause: router4’s interface eth5 toward router3 (link_r4_r3) is administratively/physically down, severing Location C from the core.

Corrective fix: Do not replace router3. Restore the link on router4: re-enable eth5 (no shutdown / ip link set eth5 up), reseat/replace the cable on link_r4_r3, or replace router4’s eth5 NIC if faulty. Verify 172.25.1.1/24 comes UP, RIP adjacency forms, routes to 172.16.10.0/24/172.16.20.0/24 appear on router4, and pings from 192.168.200.x to Location A succeed.
=== END ===